<interrogationes unitas="fabrica">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I check whether generated files and installed binaries are up to date?
    <responsum imperium="bin/fabrica iudicare -plenus" symbolum="fabrica_iudicare FabricaStatus">Run
      bin/fabrica iudicare (quick) or bin/fabrica iudicare -plenus
      (full) from the repository root, optionally naming artifacts.
      Each artifact gets RECENS, STALUM, IGNOTUM, NON IUDICATUM or
      FORTASSE with a cause. Exit 0 = all fine, 1 = something STALUM,
      IGNOTUM or FORTASSE, 2 = nothing judged (an unknown flag,
      declarations missing or broken, or no artifact matched).
      Quick mode skips regeneration unless the action is
      celer="verum", so many generated files say NON IUDICATUM; that is
      a chosen mode, not a failure, and does not affect the exit code.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does fabrica mean by "stale", and does it ever look at mtimes?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_iudicare fabrica_actionem_sigillare fabrica_ingressus_sigillare">Never
      mtimes and never "the file exists". An artifact is stale when it
      is not what its declared inputs produce TODAY. The input digest
      is, per input in path order, "path NUL sha256(bytes)". A manifest
      contributes the files it lists, not itself (it carries
      timestamps). A directory contributes its sorted name list under
      "path/" so it cannot collide with a file of the same path. The
      build/fabrica/provenientia/ directory is left out
      (fabrica_actionem_sigillare).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are the provenientia strategies (regeneratio, relatio, ignota, verdictum)?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_strategia_invenire FabricaStrategia">How
      freshness is decided per output. regeneratio: re-run the generator
      into an empty scratch directory and compare the bytes with the
      committed file. relatio: ask the installed binary for -provenientia
      and compare its "ingressus HEX" line with today's input digest.
      ignota: never judged, only realized as a precondition (cannot be
      a composite part or another action's input). verdictum: a gate's
      verdict file plus its recorded read trace, never re-run by the
      judge. fabrica_strategia_invenire looks them up by name.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I declare a new generated file so fabrica judges and heals it?
    <responsum via="aedificatio.stml aedificatio.canon fabrica.stml" symbolum="fabrica_declarationes_legere_cum_sutura fabrica_subsystemata_legere">Add
      an &lt;actio titulus genus="generator"&gt; to the subsystem's
      aedificatio.stml (subsystems are listed in fabrica.stml) with a
      &lt;mandatum&gt; of &lt;verbum! (&gt; lines (ONE argument per line,
      because a raw capture eats to end of line), &lt;ingressus genus
      via&gt; for each input, and &lt;exitus via provenientia="regeneratio"/&gt;.
      Titles must be unique across all subsystems (scratch directories
      are per title). The reader refuses unknown kinds, missing title,
      input or output, with "via:linea" in the cause.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why must my generator write into FABRICA_SCRIPTURA, and why is that directory emptied first?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_iudicare FabricaSutura" via="lib/fabrica.worklog.md tools/fabrica_sutura.c">Under
      judgment the generator runs with FABRICA_SCRIPTURA set to
      build/fabrica/scriptura/TITULUS and must write its output there
      (at the exitus 'scriptura' path, default = the output path), not
      over the committed file. The seam empties it before every run: a
      mute generator (exit 0, writes nothing) would otherwise be
      compared with the previous run's leftovers and wrongly judged
      RECENS. Nothing written there = IGNOTUM "generator nihil
      scripsit" (worklog T1).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find out why an artifact is STALUM or IGNOTUM?
    <responsum imperium="bin/fabrica causae" symbolum="FabricaIudicium fabrica_iudicare" via="tools/fabrica.c lib/fabrica.worklog.md">Read
      the cause printed next to it (FabricaIudicium.causa): "regeneratio
      differt (lineae differentes: N)", "ingressus mutati post
      institutionem", "ingressus mutatus: a, b, c +N" (N as a
      Roman numeral) or "lectio transitus mutata: X" for gate verdicts, "generator fractus: ...",
      "ingressus build/ sine domino: X". FORTASSE means the artifact
      itself matches but something above it is stale; its cause names
      the upstream root ("supra: TITULUS STATUS (cause)"). FORTASSE is
      added by bin/fabrica's chain pass (_iudicare_catenam in
      tools/fabrica.c, worklog 2026-10-15), not by fabrica_iudicare,
      which judges an artifact against its direct inputs only; a library
      caller of fabrica_iudicare never sees it. bin/fabrica causae
      [titulus] shows why actions were run.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I rebuild everything that is stale, in the right order?
    <responsum imperium="bin/fabrica sanare -siccum" symbolum="fabrica_sanare FabricaEventus fabrica_acta_via">bin/fabrica
      sanare [artifact...] walks the dependency order over the selected
      artifacts and everything above them: judge now, run the action in
      place, judge again (SANATUM or FRACTUM; a dependent of a broken
      action is OMISSUM). -siccum only reports (AGENDUM, FORTASSE). The
      log of each run is build/fabrica/acta/TITULUS.log
      (fabrica_acta_via). An action that writes outside its declared
      vestigium is FRACTUM with "scripsit extra vestigium: ...". Gate
      actions (genus iudicium) are never part of a sweep: a plain
      bin/fabrica sanare skips them, they run only when named.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test code that uses fabrica without touching the disk or running processes?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_suturam_parare FabricaSutura" via="probationes/fabrica_mundus_fictus.c tools/fabrica_sutura.c">The
      library touches disk and processes only through a FabricaSutura.
      Call fabrica_suturam_parare first (it sets EVERY member to NIHIL),
      then fill in what you provide; most members are optional and
      documented "NIHIL licet". probationes/fabrica_mundus_fictus.c is
      the in-memory world used by the tests; tools/fabrica_sutura.c is
      the real one. Skipping fabrica_suturam_parare on a stack sutura
      once left a garbage pointer in a newly added member (Bus error).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what are memorabilis and lectiones, and how do they let the judge skip a regeneration?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_liber_via fabrica_iudicare">Both
      apply to regeneratio outputs, in quick mode too (they are checked
      before the quick-mode skip). lectiones="verum": the
      generator runs with FABRICA_LECTIONES (fabrica_liber_via:
      build/fabrica/lectiones/TITULUS.tsv) and records what it read;
      if every recorded read still has the same digest, the output is
      RECENS without running. memorabilis="verum": a stored
      verification under a key of input digest plus argv is reused. Both
      are written only after a regeneration that matched. Under
      -plenus with auditus set (iudicare -audit) a hit is regenerated
      anyway, and a mismatch is reported as AUDITUM DISCORS.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can a generator with lectiones="verum" read files outside the tree and still be traced?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_iudicare fabrica_liber_via">Not
      for generators: when the read log is turned into a trace, paths
      under the tree root are made relative, and the remaining absolute
      paths (plus reads of the scratch directory, the read logs and the
      object store) are dropped, so a change to a file outside the tree
      never invalidates the trace. Declare such a file as an ingressus.
      Gate verdicts (verdictum) are stricter: absolute reads are kept
      except system and temporary roots, and reading a file under build/
      that no declared action produces makes the trace unusable (IGNOTUM
      "ingressus build/ sine domino").</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a compositum and how is its verdict computed?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_composita_legere fabrica_compositum_explicare fabrica_iudicia_coniungere fabrica_compositum_verdictum">A
      named flat list of parts (an artifact, all outputs of an action,
      or another composite) declared with &lt;compositum&gt; and
      &lt;pars&gt; elements; no expressions. fabrica_compositum_explicare
      flattens it (cycles, unknown parts and parts whose strategy is
      never judged are refused), and the verdict is
      the worst part: RECENS &lt; NON_IUDICATUM &lt; SUPRA &lt; IGNOTUM &lt;
      STALUM (fabrica_iudicia_coniungere), naming up to III of the worst
      artifacts and then "+N".</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a gradus member, the "actio/membrum" names I see in verdicts?
    <responsum imperium="bin/fabrica probare -vis" symbolum="fabrica_gradus_explicare FabricaGradus fabrica_area_via fabrica_gradus_composita">A
      gradus kind (probationes_c) expands one declared action into one
      synthetic action per member, titled "ACTIO/MEMBRUM", each with
      its own area build/fabrica/area/ACTIO/MEMBRUM/ (fabrica_area_via),
      a recorded read trace and a verdict file. The parent becomes a
      composite of its members (fabrica_gradus_composita), so a single
      changed test re-runs only that member. bin/fabrica probare
      COMPOSITUM [FILTRUM...] runs the stale members and reuses fresh
      ones; -vis re-runs every selected member.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why was a test member killed with "memoria maxima excessa"?
    <responsum symbolum="fabrica_sectiones_legere" via="tools/fabrica_sutura.h">fabrica
      runs every gradus member (link and run) with a whole-process
      memory cap: FABRICA_MEMORIA_MB from fabrica's own environment (not
      the member's), default FABRICA_MEMORIA_ORDINARIA_MB = 16 GiB, 0 =
      none. Over it the member gets SIGKILL with exit 137, and the cause
      names the footprint seen. For a sectioned credo suite the
      verdict also names the section that was running ("sectio 'X'
      RUIT"), and the sections before it keep their verdicts. The cap
      only stops runaways. A heavy section that finishes is judged by
      credo's own per-section ceiling (256 MiB, or 1024 with signum
      gravis).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
